{"reply": "**Daily Workflow Under the New Quota Rule**\n\n---\n**1. Exact Numbers**\n\n| Villager | Legal Retentive Catch (per day) | Surplus Caught | Cumulative Pot After 1\u2011Day Round |\n|----------|---------------------------------|----------------|-----------------------------------|\n| a1\u2011a6    | **1.10** units each | `Catch_i \u2013 1.10` (if positive) | Add all positive surpluses from 6 villagers | \n\n*Surplus Calculation*: If a villager\u2019s total catch `C_i` exceeds 1.10 units, the exact value of the excess (`C_i \u2013 1.10`) is transferred **immediately** to the communal pot. If `C_i \u2264 1.10`, nothing is added.\n\n**Routinely over\u2011cutter definition:**\n* A villager is flagged when their daily catch **exceeds** 1.10 units for **three consecutive days** or when their **7\u2011day moving\u2011average** of daily catch is >\u202f1.10 units. The council will review this flag after day\u202f7 and decide on action.\n\n**Surplus Redistribution Date:**\n* Surplus in the pot is held until **any day** when the **collective total** of legal catches (sum of all 6 villagers each keeping 1.10) drops below the collective 6.60\u2011unit consumption threshold *and* a villager\u2019s daily net catch (post\u2011pot distribution) would otherwise fall under 1.00 units. At that point, the pot is divided equally among the *under\u2011catching* villagers for that day.\n\n---\n**2. Roles and Timing**\n\n1. **Casting/Harvest** \u2013 *All villagers* cast their line and fish at any time during the fishing window.\n   * As soon as a villager\u2019s total catch surpasses **1.10**, the **surplus** is **hand\u2011topped** into a separate, clearly labeled container labelled \u201cSurplus Pot\u201d.\n\n2. **Daily Log\u2011Board** \u2013 *Each villager* is responsible for writing their final total catch at the end of their fishing period (i.e., after the last cast). They place a signed record on the public log\u2011board by **18:00**.\n   * The log entry format: `Villager ID \u2013 Total Catch: X.XX \u2013 Surplus: Y.YY`.\n\n3. **Council Review** \u2013 The council (any two elected villagers) meets **daily** at 18:30 to read the log\u2011board entries. The review takes **\u2248\u202f15\u202fminutes**: they total the surpluses, confirm that each villager\u2019s catch adhered to the 1.10 rule, and note any that exceeded.\n\n4. **Enforcement of Confiscation** \u2013 If the council finds a villager who exceeded 1.10, the excess amount already resides in the Surplus Pot; no further action is needed on the day. The council simply **remembers** that the excess is already counted. If a villager consistently over\u2011catches, the council will convene the **Over\u2011Cutter Meeting** on the following day\u2019s council session to set the new quota of 1.05 for them.\n\n5. **Redistribution** \u2013 The council checks the pot **once a week** on Wednesday. If any villager\u2019s *net* daily consumption (legal catch + share of pot) is below 1.00 units (after subtracting any other shared allocations), the pot is divided among those villagers **pro\u2011rata** and added to their legal 1.10. This redistributive cycle occurs **after the weekly lake\u2011biometrics audit** (every first Monday).\n\n---\n**3. Enforcement Mechanics**\n\n*Immediate Surplus Transfer:* A villager whose catch is `C_i > 1.10` immediately **donates`C_i \u2013 1.10`** to the Surplus Pot at the moment the excess is observed. No further allowance is given.\n\n*Flagging Persistent Over\u2011Cutter:* After three consecutive days over\u2011cutter or a 7\u2011day average >\u202f1.10, the council signals a **quota\u2011reduction notice**. For the next cycle (starting the following day), that villager\u2019s legal catch limit becomes **1.05** units. The rule remains the same: any catch beyond 1.05 is transferred to the pot.\n\n*Interaction with Pot:* While the surplus is stored, the villager still gets to keep 1.05 for a *sustainable* day. If the villager\u2019s actual catch is below the reduced quota, they keep the lower amount and will still **receive a portion** of the pot if the pot is disbursed that day.\n\n---\n**4. What Each Person Sacrifices**\n\n| Role | Time on Water or Catch Lost | Notes |\n|------|-----------------------------|-------|\n| Villager (normal) | 0 (no sacrifice) | They keep 1.10 or 1.05 as per quota and may get pot shares when needed. |\n| Log\u2011Keeper (any villager) | 5\u202fmin per log | Write final catch; negligible impact on catch time. |\n| Council Member | \u2248\u202f15\u202fmin daily for review | Their day can skip fishing; their catch is optional but if they fish they must still abide by quota. |\n| Enforcement (any council member) | 5\u202fmin to issue quota\u2011reduction notice | Occurs only if over\u2011cutter flagged. |\n| Pot Redistribution Officer (any council member) | 10\u202fmin weekly | Handles pot accounting and distribution. |\n\nOverall, the system requires each villager to dedicate about **20\u202fminutes** per day to record-keeping and a council member to spend an additional **15\u202fminutes** reviewing logs. The enforcement and redistribution steps each take less than **10\u202fminutes** in a typical village, so the net time cost is minimal compared to the benefits of steady food supply and lake sustainability.\n\n---\n**Summary of Workflow**\n1. Fish (up to 1.10/1.05). 2. If over\u2011cutter, transfer surplus to pot immediately. 3. At day\u2019s end, log total and surplus. 4. Council reviews logs by 18:30. 5. Weekly pot redistribution if needed.\n\nThis procedure gives us a transparent, quick\u2011actionable system that protects each villager\u2019s survival while ensuring the lake remains healthy for future generations.\n"}
