You proposed this policy to the community: "We will shift the community policy to a flexible “core‑plus‑surplus” system: every villager keeps a guaranteed core of 1.00 unit for survival.  They may aim for a personal maximum of 1.30 units, which covers a moderate competitive edge while keeping the lake’s biomass stable.  Any catch over 1.30 units is sent into the communal pot.  The pot is then *redistributed* only on days when the *aggregate* lake catch falls below the collective 6.60‑unit threshold.  This means that on high‑yield days the pot remains intact, boosting each villager’s personal haul, while on low‑yield days the community shares the buffer stock to guarantee everyone at least 1.00 unit.  The council monitors total catch but only steps in if a villager consistently exceeds the 1.30 limit for more than two consecutive days.  If long‑term biomass metrics show a decline, all villagers will agree to lower the personal limit to 1.20 units, keeping the surplus to the pot.  This policy balances individual competitiveness (our community can still out‑fish each other within limits) with collective sustainability and guarantees a safety net for all."

