{"reply": "Dear Council, thank you for the opportunity to clarify the practical details of our policy. Below are the concrete procedures I propose, in line with the community guidelines:\n\n**1. Recording & Cross\u2011Checking of Daily Catch**\n- **Recorder role**: Each day, every villager logs his/her own catch immediately on a shared digital ledger (or on a physical whiteboard at the village hall).\n- **Primary verifier**: The villager who logs the catch hands the ledger to *their assigned verifier*, which changes each day (e.g., on day\u202f1 a4 verifies a1\u2019s catch, day\u202f2 a5 verifies a2\u2019s catch, etc.). The verifier checks the recorded number against their own observation of the catch (e.g., by a quick visual count or by noting the weight on the scale). If any discrepancy is found, the two villagers discuss and adjust the entry.\n- **Finalization**: Once verified, the ledger entry is signed (or given a unique QR code) by both the recorder and the verifier. The completed ledger is posted on the community board (or uploaded to a cloud folder) before 18:00 daily. Only after the ledger is fully signed and posted does the council officially log the day\u2019s totals.\n\n**2. Determining \u201cConsistent Exceedance\u201d of the 1.10\u2011unit Limit**\n- **Threshold**: A villager is considered to have *consistently exceeded* the quota if they record more than 1.10 units for **three consecutive days**.\n- **Evidence**: For each day of excess, the verifier\u2019s signed ledger must be preserved. The council will audit the last 3\u2011day block at the next council meeting, checking:\n  1. The recorder\u2019s logged number.\n  2. The verifier\u2019s signature (or QR\u2011code verification).\n  3. The official community ledger entry posted that day.\n- If all three days show a >\u202f1.10 value and the verifier\u2019s cross\u2011check confirms it, the council has concrete proof of consistent exceedance.\n\n**3. Corrective Measures Once Threshold is Met**\n- **Immediate action**: The council will declare the villager\u2019s quota *temporarily reduced* to **1.05 units** for the next **7 consecutive days**. This reduction is documented on the ledger and announced on the community board.\n- **Fine**: In addition to the quota reduction, the villager must transfer **10\u202f% of any surplus** caught during that 7\u2011day period into the communal pot. For example, if the villager catches 1.20 units on day\u202f1, 0.20 units are considered surplus; 10\u202f% of that (0.02 units) is fined to the pot.\n- **Communication**: The council will publicly note the new quota and fine in the daily update that is shared with all villagers (e.g., a brief post on the shared board). The affected villager will receive a written notice (via the community messenger app) summarizing the new quota, the fine amount, and the enforcement period.\n- **Enforcement**: The ledger will reflect the reduced quota automatically. The village watch (assigned randomly each day) will observe the fishing activity and report any further non\u2011compliance. After the 7\u2011day period, the council will review the ledger: if the villager\u2019s total within those days averaged \u2264\u202f1.10, the penalty is lifted; otherwise a further action (e.g., a second 7\u2011day reduction) may be considered.\n\nThese steps align with our shared policy and ensure transparency, fairness, and sustainability. I remain committed to following and supporting the council\u2019s oversight. Please let me know if any part needs further adjustment.\n"}
