3. **Enforcement and penalty mechanism** – If a villager consistently (≥3 days in a row) exceeds the 1.10 limit, the council will impose a fine equal to 5 % of the surplus he/she caught that day. The fine is added to a community fund that can be used for fishery‑related improvements (e.g., better nets, monitoring devices). Persistent non‑compliance will trigger a temporary suspension from fishing duties.
4. **Adaptive quota‑decrease mechanism** – If lake biometrics (e.g., fish density, spawning rates) show a decline of more than 5 % over two consecutive seasons, the council will convene a vote in which *all* villagers agree to lower the personal quota to 1.05 units. The same surplus‑pot policy will apply.
5. **Transparency & education** – The council will publish a quarterly report on lake health and individual compliance. We will also hold a monthly community gathering to discuss best practices, share any surplus responsibly, and keep the tradition of “sharing the over‑catch” alive.

**Why this works** – The rules keep fishing levels below the environmental carrying capacity, protect each villager’s survival threshold, and incentivize honest reporting while providing a safety net for those who fall below 1.00 units. It also builds a culture of shared responsibility and long‑term stewardship of the lake.

I believe this policy will preserve the lake for future generations while ensuring we all meet our daily needs."

