{"reply": "Good morning Council, thank you for asking for more clarity. To keep the system simple, transparent, and in line with our goal of sustaining the lake, I propose the following concrete mechanics for the **daily collective reporting ledger**.\n\n---\n**1. Who runs the ledger?**\n- We will appoint a *Rotating Compliance Officer (RCO)*. Every day the RCO will be the villager whose catch is last in the calendar order (a1 \u279c a2 \u279c \u2026 \u279c a6 \u279c a1\u2026). This guarantees that every person sits in that role for the same amount of time (one shift per week) and eliminates the need for a permanent staffer.\n- The RCO will have a simple two\u2011step task: (i) gather written or electronic submissions from each villager; (ii) transcribe data into the shared ledger.\n\n**2. How much time does the RCO need?**\n- Each villager submits a short, standardized snapshot: {date, name, effort, catch, surplus (if any), notes}. The RCO will spend roughly **15 minutes** in the evening, once everyone has sent in their data. Because the information is a few numbers, transcription into the ledger is fast.\n\n**3. What data is logged?**\n- Date (day of season)\n- Villager name\n- Effort (hours or net\u2011units)\n- Raw catch (kg or units)\n- Surplus (catch \u2013 1.10, if >0)\n- Verification flag (yes/no \u2014 see point 4)\n- Short note (any fishery\u2011health observation, e.g., \"seen more young fish today\")\n- Sign\u2011off (RCO initials)\n\n**4. Verification mechanism**\n- Each morning a *random audit* will be conducted by the monitoring board (not the RCO). They will spot\u2011check 2\u20133 random entries each day against the net logs or trap counts that the fisherman keeps on their boat (we already keep a simple log for our own records). If more than 10\u202f% of the entries are inconsistent, the council will review the ledger with that day\u2019s RCO.\n- The RCO will also keep a side\u2011by\u2011side copy of the raw submissions as PDF or paper, so the audit trail is clear.\n- Because the audit is light and performed by the monitoring board, it imposes only a few minutes per day, well under the 15\u2011minute compilation window.\n\n**5. Where is the ledger stored?**\n- We will use a shared, cloud\u2011based spreadsheet (e.g., Google Sheets) with a lock on the main tab and a draft tab for the RCO. This allows everyone to see the data instantly, ensures transparency, and gives the council a real\u2011time view. The spreadsheet will have built\u2011in checks (e.g., if surplus > 0, the field auto\u2011calculates). If a villager\u2019s surplus is shown, an automatic flag will appear for audit.\n\n**6. Timing**\n- All entries should be submitted by **7\u202fpm** local time. The RCO's compilation window is 7\u202fpm \u2013 7:15\u202fpm. The council reviews the final ledger the next morning at 8\u202fam.\n\n---\nThis rotation keeps workload low, eliminates a dedicated administrative position, ensures every villager is equally accountable for data entry, and gives the council a quick, verifiable snapshot of our collective consumption. It also aligns with our stewardship ethos: minimal bureaucratic overhead, maximum transparency, and a real\u2011time health check on the lake. \n\nI am happy to discuss any tweaks or to pilot this on a trial week. Thank you for considering this practical approach for safeguarding our shared lake for future generations.\n\n\u2014 a5"}
