{"reply": "Hello Counselor,\n\nThank you for asking about the practical side of keeping reliable daily records and how council verification will work. Below is a simple, transparent, and low\u2011technology system that fits our community\u2019s size and culture, while giving the council the checks it needs to enforce the surplus\u2011pot policy.\n\n1. **Daily Catch Log (Personal Ledger)**\n   - Every fisher keeps a small, hand\u2011written \u201ccatch note\u201d on a dedicated sheet of paper (or a simple notebook).  The note contains:\n     1. Date\n     2. Name (or ID letter)\n     3. Weight of fish caught that day (in decimal\u2011units, e.g.,\u202f1.03)\n     4. Immediately after the catch, the fisher writes a short code that shows whether the catch was *\u2264\u202f1.10* or *>\u202f1.10* (e.g., \u201c+\u201d for surplus, \u201c\u2013\u201d for none).\n     5. A signature/cross\u2011check by a randomly assigned \u201cwatcher\u201d from another fisher that day (rotated weekly).\n   - We keep a copy of each fisher\u2019s note in a communal ledger that sits in the village hall.  Each fisher also brings the printed slip to the council meeting at the end of the day.\n\n2. **Council\u2011Daily Tally Sheet**\n   - The council keeps a master sheet that lists every fisher\u2019s daily catch figure.  On arrival, each fisher hands over the handwritten slip, and the councillor confirms the value by reading a second independent \u201cwatcher\u201d copy.\n   - The councillor records the sum of all daily catches.  If this total equals or exceeds 6.60 units for that day, the council immediately deposits any surplus beyond 1.10 units per person into the communal pot the same evening.  If the total falls below 6.60, the pot will not be touched.\n\n3. **Verification Protocol (Random Audits & Spot Checks)**\n   - Each day, the council selects one fisher at random (or works in rotational pairs) to perform a *spot\u2011check*: they weigh the fish on the spot using a simple kitchen scale or a yardstick\u2011based estimation, and verify that the weight matches the value on the daily log.\n   - In addition, at the end of each week the council cross\u2011checks the cumulative totals in the individual ledgers against the master sheet.  Any discrepancy triggers a discussion and, if necessary, a re\u2011measure or a community\u2011resolution meeting.\n\n4. **Handling Surplus & Withdrawal**\n   - Surplus fish is physically transferred to a communal pot (a basket or small container) that sits under council protection.  Only the council counts and records how many units of surplus are in the pot each day it receives fish.\n   - When the weekly or monthly *aggregate* across all days dips below 6.60 units of total fish, the council will open the pot and distribute the accumulated units to the fishers who have been short that week \u2013 ensuring everyone gets at least the daily requirement of 1.00 units.\n\n5. **Transparency & Community Trust**\n   - All logs, tally sheets, and pot deposits are left in an open area of the village hall for all fishers to see.  No single person owns the records.  The \u201cwatcher\u201d system means each fisher\u2019s log is observed by a peer, reducing tampering.\n   - We will hold a quarterly \u201cRecord\u2011Keeping Review\u201d meeting where we all look at the past month\u2019s cumulative logs, confirm the pot\u2019s balance, and discuss any needed adjustments to our policy or procedures.\n\nThis system is simple enough for daily use, requires no expensive equipment, and gives the council the audit trail it needs to verify records quickly.  By involving every fisher through the watcher rotation and by visibly sharing all tallies in the hall, we build the trust necessary for the surplus\u2011pot approach to work.\n\nI hope this clarifies how we can maintain reliable daily records and ensure council verification before any fish is moved to, or withdrawn from, the communal pot. Happy to discuss any part of the process in more detail.\n\n\u2014 a6"}
